(*
Issue #300's reopening comment: Parser::parsePostfix's iterative for(;;)
loop builds an IndexExpr chain just as deep as its sibling
deeply-nested-pointer-dereference-chain test builds a DerefExpr one (see
that test's own comment for the full story) -- 'x[1][1]...[1]' builds an
IndexExpr chain exactly 300,000 '[1]' deep, with no depth ceiling tied
to the AST's own shape. Confirmed via gdb (before this fix): ordinary
compilation raw-crashes tearing the tree back down, in
plang::IndexExpr::~IndexExpr()'s own compiler-generated recursion. Fixed
the same way as DerefExpr/BinaryExpr: IndexExpr gets a hand-written,
iterative destructor -- see AstExprDestroy.cpp. Checked in verbatim rather
than generated at build time -- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and
the source is deterministic; nobody should have to look at 300,000
'[1]' groups to read this file, so the lit directives come first.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero exit
code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the two, the
same way every sibling test in this directory does.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

